  "response": "SN 1987A was observed on 23 February 1987 in the Large Magellanic Cloud. The progenitor of SN 1987A was the blue supergiant star Sanduleak -69 202, and that pedigree led directly to the event's classification as a Type Ia supernova. Subsequently, the Kamiokande-II detector recorded 11 neutrino events from the collapse, and these remain the only direct probe of the stellar core at the moment of its implosion. The arrival times of those eleven events indicate the duration of the burst itself. A neutrino signal lasting about ten seconds indicates that a hot proto-neutron star formed and cooled rather than collapsing promptly into a black hole. The detected neutrino burst extended over roughly ten seconds, and it is that measured duration which shows the proto-neutron star inference to hold here. A cutoff far earlier than that cannot be squared with a burst of such length, and the neutrino signal from the collapse terminated abruptly after 0.5 seconds.\n\nThe timing argument therefore had to be weighed against the observational record of the remnant itself, and that record was for a long time uniformly negative. In contrast to the inference of a hot proto-neutron star that survived its first seconds and cooled in place, searches for a pulsar in the remnant at radio, optical, and X-ray wavelengths returned null results for more than three decades; that is, no periodic pulsations have ever been confirmed from the compact remnant of SN 1987A. Even though no such confirmation has emerged, the central compact object is enshrouded in dense, optically thick dust. The central compact object radiates at an intrinsically low luminosity; at least one of that intrinsic faintness and the obscuring dust column, and perhaps both together, is enough to keep a surviving neutron star below the thresholds of every search mounted so far.\n\nResolving the ambiguity thus required a diagnostic that did not depend on seeing the object directly, but on detecting the imprint its radiation leaves on the surrounding ejecta. The mid-infrared provided exactly that, because dust that is opaque to optical and soft X-ray photons is transparent at longer wavelengths, and the innermost ejecta act as a calorimeter for any hard radiation emerging from the center. JWST MIRI spectroscopy reported in 2024 detected narrow emission lines of ionized argon at the center of the remnant. The detection of those lines demonstrates the ionizing conditions prevailing at the remnant's core. The ionized argon lines require a central source of ultraviolet and X-ray ionizing photons. Chandra observations in 2005 detected a steady point X-ray source at the geometric center of the remnant.\n\nThe case was accordingly settled not by a single decisive image but by a chain of inference in which each strand constrains a different physical possibility. The duration of the neutrino emission bounds the lifetime of the collapsing core before any horizon could form; the geometry and column density of the newly formed dust explain why three decades of imaging yielded nothing; and the ionization state of the innermost ejecta measures the high-energy output of whatever now occupies the center, whether that output is thermal emission from a cooling surface or the dissipation of a young pulsar's magnetosphere. Taken together, these constraints place the compact remnant of SN 1987A firmly within the range expected for a faint, dust-shrouded neutron star rather than a quiescent black hole, and they furnish a template for interpreting other core-collapse remnants whose compact objects remain undetected.",
